1) What is UAT? Who will do the testing and what is the purpose of testing?

   UAT (User Acceptance Testing) is the final phase of testing done before a software product goes live.

   Who does it: The end users or clients (not developers or testers) — The actual people who will use the software in real life.
                Sometimes a dedicated UAT team or business representatives do it on behalf of end users.

   Purpose:  To verify that the software meets business requirements and works correctly in real-world scenarios,
             from the user's point of view — not just that it's technically bug-free.
   

2) What is the difference between Functional and Non functional testing? 


          Functional Testing                                                    Non-Functional Testing

     Tests what the system does                                            Tests how well the system performs

    Based on requirements/specifications                                     Based on quality attributes

    Checks features, business logic                                          Checks performance, usability, security

    Example: Does login work with correct credentials?                       Example: How fast does login happen with 1000  users ? 

    Functional = behavior                                                    Non-functional = quality of behavior.



3) Load testing will come under functional or non functional testing? And why it is coming under that category?

       Load Testing comes under Non-Functional Testing.
   
Why: Load testing doesn't check what the system does (like a feature working correctly) — 
It checks how the system performs/behaves under a specific expected load (e.g., how the app performs when 500 users log in at the same time). 
Since it's about performance, speed, and stability rather than functionality/features, 
it falls under non-functional testing (specifically under Performance Testing).


4) What is Exploratory Testing and when we will do this testing?

    Exploratory Testing is a testing approach where the tester simultaneously learns the application, designs tests, and executes them — without following pre-written test cases or scripts.
    The tester explores the application freely based on their experience and intuition to find defects.


   When it's done:

    When documentation/requirements are incomplete or unclear.
    Early in development, when there isn't time to write formal test cases.
    Alongside scripted testing, to catch bugs that structured test cases might miss.
    When testers want to think like a real user and try unexpected paths.


5)  What is Black box Testing and how it differs from White box testing?

    Black Box Testing:

    Testing the software without knowing its internal code/structure.
    The tester only looks at inputs and outputs — like a "black box" where you can't see inside.

    Focus: Functionality, based on requirements
    Done by: Testers (no programming knowledge needed)
    Techniques: Equivalence partitioning, boundary value analysis.

    
    White Box Testing:
    
  Testing the software with full knowledge of the internal code/structure.
  The tester checks the internal logic, code paths, and structures.

  Focus: Internal logic — the techniques you studied recently (Statement, Branch, Path, Condition Coverage)
  Done by: Developers or testers with coding knowledge
  Techniques: Code coverage analysis.












   

   
